You are working in the repository checked out at /dayjs (the dayjs JavaScript date library, jest + babel). Your job: FIX two real bugs in /dayjs/src/plugin/timezone/index.js and verify them. Do not modify any other tracked file (especially not /dayjs/test/**).

Background diagnostics already done (trust these):
- The container TZ is UTC.
- `dayjs.tz(input, timezone)` (the `d.tz` function, ~lines 60-66) always treats `input` as a wall-clock string: it does `const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()` then `fixOffset`, so absolute instants (number timestamp, JS Date, Day.js object, moment object) get shifted by the zone offset. Example: `dayjs.tz(1401638400000, 'America/New_York').valueOf()` returns 1401652800000 but moment-timezone returns 1401638400000 (expected `2014-06-01T12:00:00-04:00`).
- `proto.tz` (~lines 55-59) computes `const diff = (this.toDate() - new Date(target)) / 1000 / 60` which is FRACTIONAL when `this.$ms !== 0` (target string from toLocaleString has no milliseconds). That makes `$offset` fractional, so `format('Z')` renders garbage like `-04:0.0020499999999969987` instead of `-04:00`, and the milliseconds are dropped from the wall clock. Reproduce with `dayjs().tz('America/New_York').format('Z')` (the test file's beforeEach does `MockDate.set(new Date())`, so ms are non-zero).

REQUIRED FIXES (edit /dayjs/src/plugin/timezone/index.js only):
1. In `d.tz`, before the string path, add a non-string branch:
```js
  d.tz = function (input, timezone) {
    const previousOffset = tzOffset(+d(), timezone)
    if (typeof input !== 'string') {
      // timestamp, date, dayjs
      return d(input).tz(timezone)
    }
    const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()
    ...
```
2. In `proto.tz`, round the diff to whole minutes and preserve milliseconds:
```js
  proto.tz = function (timezone) {
    const target = this.toDate().toLocaleString('en-US', { timeZone: timezone })
    const diff = Math.round((this.toDate() - new Date(target)) / 1000 / 60)
    return d(target).$set(MS, this.$ms).utcOffset(localUtcOffset - diff, true)
  }
```
Note: `MS` is the constant exported by /dayjs/src/constant.js — you must import it at the top of the plugin file (`import { MS } from '../../constant'`) and add `MS` to the plugin's exported constants object if the file has one (check the file's existing style; the utc plugin imports constants similarly). Verify `$set` exists on the Dayjs prototype in /dayjs/src/index.js before using it.

